---
import Article from '../../layouts/Article.astro'
import AssertionCard from '../../components/AssertionCard.astro'
import ChartFigure from '../../components/ChartFigure.astro'
import Gloss from '../../components/Gloss.astro'
import { assertion } from '../../lib/feeds'
import { bar } from '../../lib/chart-spec'

const rate = assertion('one-renter-household-in-fifteen-is-sued')
const flat = assertion('the-eviction-count-does-not-move')
const disp = assertion('busy-eviction-years-with-no-direction')
const rank = assertion('below-the-state-and-high-on-its-list')
const serial = assertion('a-filing-is-not-a-household')

const spec = (a: typeof rate, yTitle: string) =>
  bar(
    a.figures.map((f) => f.label),
    a.figures.map((f) => f.value),
    { yTitle, labelValues: true },
  )

const rateSpec = spec(rate, 'Filings per 100 renter households')
const flatSpec = spec(flat, 'Eviction filings in a year')
const dispSpec = spec(disp, 'Standard deviation, filings')
const serialSpec = spec(serial, 'Count, 2018')
---

<Article slug="one-in-fifteen">
  <p>
    What renters in this county pay is well covered here: 35.7 per cent of them hand over more than
    a third of their income for housing, and 17.4 per cent more than half. What happens to the ones
    who cannot has been a blank, because it happens in a courthouse and nothing in the corpus reached
    inside one. Eviction filings, county by county and year by year, close that.
  </p>
  <AssertionCard assertion={rate} open />
  <ChartFigure
    id="rate"
    title="Eviction filings per hundred renter households"
    caption="Allen County, 2002 to 2018. The denominator is renter households, interpolated between census and survey vintages, so it moves smoothly every year."
    columns={['Year', 'Rate']}
    rows={rate.figures.map((f) => [f.label, `${f.value.toFixed(2)}%`])}
  />
  <script type="application/json" data-chart-spec="rate" set:html={JSON.stringify(rateSpec)} is:inline />
  <p>
    Roughly one renter household in fifteen is sued for possession each year. That is the sharp end
    of the cost-burden figures rather than a separate fact about the place — and it is worth being
    clear that being sued is not the same as being put out, which the file cannot see.
  </p>

  <h2>A number that stays where it is</h2>
  <p>
    The obvious expectation is that eviction tracks the economy: more in a downturn, fewer when work
    is plentiful, and a step somewhere around the housing crash. None of that is in the series. The
    highest year is 2008 and the lowest is 2017, and the distance between them is two hundred and
    sixteen cases.
  </p>
  <AssertionCard assertion={flat} />
  <ChartFigure
    id="flat"
    title="Seventeen years of filings"
    caption="Lowest, mean and highest. The years in between include the foreclosure crisis, the recession that followed it, and a rise of a quarter in the county's renter households."
    columns={['Measure', 'Filings']}
    rows={flat.figures.map((f) => [f.label, f.value.toLocaleString('en-US')])}
  />
  <script type="application/json" data-chart-spec="flat" set:html={JSON.stringify(flatSpec)} is:inline />
  <p>
    Eight hundred and sixty-five a year, give or take fifty. Whatever moved in this county's housing
    between 2002 and 2018 — and a great deal did — the number of households taken to court over rent
    did not move with it.
  </p>
  <Gloss>
    Which is a claim, and claims of that shape are the easy ones to get away with. So it comes with
    its own arithmetic. Fitted against the year, the filing <em>rate</em> falls 0.058 points
    annually, which is 0.92 points across the run on a standard error of 0.024. Two standard errors
    is 0.76 points across the run — so these seventeen readings could have found a drift of about
    eight tenths of a point and no smaller, and the drift they found is nine tenths. The count is
    flat; the rate is very slightly not; and a node that says <em>nothing changed</em> without
    naming the smallest change it could have seen is not saying anything.
  </Gloss>
  <AssertionCard assertion={disp} />
  <ChartFigure
    id="disp"
    title="How much a flat series still moves"
    caption="Observed year-to-year spread against the spread a Poisson process at the same mean would produce. Real variation, no direction."
    columns={['Source of spread', 'Filings']}
    rows={disp.figures.map((f) => [f.label, `${f.value.toFixed(0)}`])}
  />
  <script type="application/json" data-chart-spec="disp" set:html={JSON.stringify(dispSpec)} is:inline />
  <p>
    So the series is not smooth — it moves about twice as much year to year as a random process at
    the same average would — but the movement has no direction in it. There are busy eviction years
    in this county and quiet ones, and knowing which one you are in tells you nothing about the next.
  </p>

  <h2>Two true sentences</h2>
  <AssertionCard assertion={rank} />
  <p>
    Allen County's filing rate sits below Ohio's and in the top fifth of Ohio's eighty-seven
    reporting counties. Both statements come out of the same column. The state figure divides all
    the filings by all the renter households, so Cuyahoga and Franklin dominate it and they have high
    rates; the ranking gives Paulding and Cuyahoga one place each. Below average and near the top of
    the list are not in tension — they are answers to different questions, and a page that prints
    one without the other has chosen a mood.
  </p>
  <AssertionCard assertion={serial} />
  <ChartFigure
    id="serial"
    title="Cases and households, 2018"
    caption="The gap is repeat filings against a household already sued that year. Across the run the ratio runs from 1.100 to 1.195."
    columns={['Measure', 'Count']}
    rows={serial.figures.map((f) => [f.label, f.value.toLocaleString('en-US')])}
  />
  <script type="application/json" data-chart-spec="serial" set:html={JSON.stringify(serialSpec)} is:inline />
  <p>
    Three quantities live under the word <em>eviction</em>: cases filed, households sued, and
    judgments granted. This page reports the first two and stops. How many people actually left a
    home in Allen County is not in any of the three files, and saying so is the honest end of a page
    about people losing their housing.
  </p>
</Article>
